WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.066
Secure C code must handle resources
consistently on both success and failure paths.

2
00:00:09.146 --> 00:00:16.264
Resources include memory, files, file
descriptors, sockets, locks,

3
00:00:16.344 --> 00:00:24.911
temporary files, handles, mapped regions,
and dynamically allocated structures.

4
00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:30.661
Every acquisition should have a matching
release path.

5
00:00:30.741 --> 00:00:37.136
Error paths need the same cleanup
discipline as success paths.

6
00:00:37.216 --> 00:00:45.420
Many defects appear when a function
succeeds through the common path but leaks or

7
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.567
double-cleans resources when a later step
fails.

8
00:00:50.647 --> 00:00:55.473
A function that allocates memory, opens a
file,

9
00:00:55.553 --> 00:01:03.637
and then fails halfway through should
leave the process in a predictable state.

10
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:08.784
Partial initialization needs careful
handling.

11
00:01:08.864 --> 00:01:15.862
If a structure owns several resources and
only some are initialized,

12
00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:20.406
cleanup code must know which ones are
valid.

13
00:01:20.486 --> 00:01:25.915
Consistent initialization, clear ownership
flags,

14
00:01:25.995 --> 00:01:31.907
and ordered cleanup can help avoid leaks,
double cleanup,

15
00:01:31.987 --> 00:01:35.365
and inconsistent return values.

16
00:01:35.445 --> 00:01:43.649
Cleanup sections can be useful when they
make ownership and release order clear.

17
00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:49.158
The point is not a particular style for
every project.

18
00:01:49.238 --> 00:01:57.804
The point is that reviewers can see one
path where resources are released correctly,

19
00:01:57.884 --> 00:02:02.107
including after intermediate failures.

20
00:02:02.187 --> 00:02:09.909
Temporary files and output files need safe
names, permissions, retention,

21
00:02:09.989 --> 00:02:12.281
and cleanup behavior.

22
00:02:12.361 --> 00:02:18.394
A temporary file may contain sensitive
intermediate data.

23
00:02:18.474 --> 00:02:22.576
An output file may be partially written.

24
00:02:22.656 --> 00:02:30.498
Code should define whether partial output
is removed, replaced atomically,

25
00:02:30.578 --> 00:02:36.972
marked incomplete, or handled through
another safe workflow.

26
00:02:37.052 --> 00:02:45.136
Error messages should be useful but should
not expose secrets, internal paths,

27
00:02:45.216 --> 00:02:48.473
raw input, or sensitive memory.

28
00:02:48.553 --> 00:02:56.999
Diagnostic detail for authorized operators
should be separated from user-facing

29
00:02:57.079 --> 00:02:58.165
messages.

30
00:02:58.245 --> 00:03:04.760
A safe error path releases resources,
preserves a clear status,

31
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.580
and avoids unnecessary disclosure.
